&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:36:56.265 --> 00:37:02.463
Is there another question?

00:37:02.463 --> 00:37:05.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't, I

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:11.880
don't know about the mistake word, I
thought.

00:37:14.580 --> 00:37:15.720
I.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:16.910
Every time I get.

00:37:16.910 --> 00:37:18.160
You're reflecting about this all the time.

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:19.370
What, work you've done.

00:37:19.370 --> 00:37:19.750
When.

00:37:19.750 --> 00:37:21.580
Or, actions you have taken.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:26.840
And, it was, And, there's not anything
that I would not

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:30.190
do, I think, part of the reason is, we're
in crisis, right?

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:30.820
And so, you have to.

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:31.880
You have to pull together.

00:37:31.880 --> 00:37:33.920
You have to decide what you're going to
do.

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:37.620
Together and then you've gotta move
decisively and,

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:39.350
I don't know how we could've moved any
faster.

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:42.390
I'm so pleased about how we pulled
together.

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:44.780
I'll tell you, another thing, I wondered,
really wondered whether I was doing

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:49.050
the right thing by going back and
testifying on behalf of GM and Chrysler.

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:52.170
I mean, I'm a market person.

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:54.699
I really believe that market should
allocate precious resources.

00:37:55.970 --> 00:37:57.570
Not governments, the market.

00:37:57.570 --> 00:37:59.390
We should do it with our purchase
decision, right?

00:37:59.390 --> 00:38:01.268
We're the ones that decide the products
are,

00:38:01.268 --> 00:38:04.310
are successful or what companies will be
successful.

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:09.720
And so to go back and testify on behalf of
my

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:14.420
bankrupt competitors, I mean, it's a, it's
a serious question, right?

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:16.260
They were, they were gone they should have

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:18.350
been gone the market should have let it
go.

00:38:18.350 --> 00:38:22.830
But I also at the same time believed like
two administrations and all their

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:25.920
economic advisors that if we would have
taken 12 to 15% of the US

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.590
GDP in the bankruptcy in the United States
we would have taken the United

00:38:29.590 --> 00:38:33.779
States from a recession into a depression
so that's why I decided to do it.

00:38:34.830 --> 00:38:36.240
And if you like to know how to go to a

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:38.435
hearing, most important thing is to fly on
your corporate jet.

00:38:38.435 --> 00:38:44.918
[LAUGH]
Works really well.

00:38:44.918 --> 00:38:50.700
I kept reminding them I was not there to
ask for the precious taxpayer

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:55.160
money, I was there supporting my, my
competitor for the good of, of the world.

00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:57.160
And the other thing is, is to have

00:38:57.160 --> 00:38:59.720
very thoughtful answers to their questions
is another thing.

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:03.070
Do you remember when, when we were, we
were

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:05.650
all sitting there and they were going down
asking,

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:08.234
Chrysler, would you, the leader, would you
work for

00:39:08.234 --> 00:39:09.525
$1 a year, if we give you our money.

00:39:09.525 --> 00:39:10.600
Tax-payer money.

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:12.250
Yes, please, thank you.

00:39:12.250 --> 00:39:13.353
Would you work for $1 a year.

00:39:13.353 --> 00:39:13.853
Yes.

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:18.230
And,

00:39:20.530 --> 00:39:20.930
please.

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:23.828
And then he turned around to me and said

00:39:23.828 --> 00:39:27.210
well Mr. [UNKNOWN] would you work for $1
per year?

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:29.150
I, I just couldn't think of what to say.

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:30.670
So I kind of rubbed my chin thoughtfully
and

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:31.557
I said, no I think I'm okay where I am.

00:39:31.557 --> 00:39:32.710
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:34.065
Just got killed for that.

00:39:34.065 --> 00:39:35.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:39.210
&gt;&gt; When I reminded them that I wasn't
there to ask them

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:42.140
for them for the money, but they kind of
forgot about that.

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:42.665
Whatever.

00:39:42.665 --> 00:39:45.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:45.210 --> 00:39:49.280
&gt;&gt; The other thing is, is they you know,
you're sitting there and they give

00:39:49.280 --> 00:39:52.760
you little bottles of water and the most
important thing is just to sip it.

00:39:52.760 --> 00:39:53.775
Cos you can't get up and leave.

00:39:53.775 --> 00:39:57.668
[LAUGH]
And we're there sometimes like 12 hours.

00:39:57.668 --> 00:40:01.720
And so that's another important thing is
going really easy on the water.

00:40:03.265 --> 00:40:04.715
Cuz the whole worlds watching you.

00:40:04.715 --> 00:40:14.030
[LAUGH]
Now on a, on a really serious note.

00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:15.780
And I think about this all the time.

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:19.080
A lot of people ask me, cuz this is,

00:40:19.080 --> 00:40:22.410
you know, it's democracy and it's the way
it works.

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:25.970
And weren't you offended at the way you,
you were treated?

00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:30.010
and, and cuz, you know, they were pretty
aggressive questions.

00:40:30.010 --> 00:40:31.300
It was a serious situation.

00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:33.369
The United States was slowing down
economically.

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:38.320
Here you had a significant portion of the
GDP was at risk.

00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:40.410
And it's going to get worse.

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:45.740
And Hank Paulson, who is secretary of the
treasury was sitting in the same chair

00:40:45.740 --> 00:40:47.170
that I was sitting in the day before

00:40:47.170 --> 00:40:49.342
trying to explain where the $700 billion
went.

00:40:49.342 --> 00:40:52.680
And everybody was scared...

00:40:52.680 --> 00:40:53.920
And, they're anxious.

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:56.240
And, they're asking questions that need to
be asked.

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:59.490
And, so I never took it, I never took it
personally.

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:02.320
And, I also believe, the only way to, to
deal with

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:04.230
the, with things like this, is you gotta
do it together.

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:04.950
You gotta make it okay.

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:07.020
Just like we were talking about, to have a
conversation.

00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:08.790
So, I never, I don't have any remorse for
that.

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:10.380
And, I, I wouldn't have done that
differently.

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:12.340
I think, everything on the product was
good.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:15.955
So I don't think of it so much the

00:41:15.955 --> 00:41:20.340
mistakes as that, I think that just
pulling together around

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:23.430
this compelling plan we decisively, that
we, that we did

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:25.220
it at the right time and, and doing it
fast.

00:41:25.220 --> 00:41:26.130
And it doesn't get any better.

00:41:26.130 --> 00:41:28.450
You know, most people tell you that, they

00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:30.840
wish they would've done something sooner,
not waited.

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:33.770
I think maybe because we were in such a
terrible crisis, we had to move.

00:41:35.370 --> 00:41:40.100
And I'm in awe that, that we moved so fast
and we're now

00:41:40.100 --> 00:41:43.140
making the best cars and trucks in the
world that people really do want.

00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:45.630
Yes sir.

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:48.548
&gt;&gt; My name's Marcus and thank you for your
time today.

00:41:48.548 --> 00:41:50.260
My questions about vision.

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:53.420
So you talked a little bit about Ford's
globalization.

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:53.610
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:41:53.610 --> 00:41:54.800
&gt;&gt; And a lot of your competitors have

00:41:54.800 --> 00:41:57.250
opened up plants in the United States
so...

00:41:57.250 --> 00:42:02.240
My question is, what does it mean to be an
American car manufacturer?

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:02.957
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:42:02.957 --> 00:42:05.680
It kind of goes back to the question
earlier.

00:42:07.650 --> 00:42:10.400
And I only can kind of speak from a, a

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:14.980
multinational, large corporation operates
around the

00:42:14.980 --> 00:42:17.460
world with Boeing and with Ford.

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:20.860
And it really, is about being a global
company.

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:23.330
And, you can have your headquarters
somewhere.

00:42:23.330 --> 00:42:24.460
You know the taxes and the way

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:28.560
you repatriate money, exchange rates and
everything.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:30.270
But I really believe that the companies
that

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.860
are gonna serve and provide what people
really do

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:35.190
want, and do it most effectively are gonna

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:38.030
be one that lav, leverage their assets
world wide.

00:42:38.030 --> 00:42:41.670
And you know, some brands are.

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:44.540
There's always that conversation about is,
is Amer, is the fact

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:47.240
that it's an American brand, does that
help you or not?

00:42:47.240 --> 00:42:51.160
The neat thing about Ford, again, is that
when Henry set it

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:54.330
up everybody around the world thinks about
their Ford operation as Ford.

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:56.630
You know, Ford is number one, number two.

00:42:56.630 --> 00:42:58.380
All the markets in Europe and it, and Ford
of Britain

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:03.560
concerned to be a British company and so
we're very fortunate.

00:43:03.560 --> 00:43:06.030
And that's very different and like you
said the company's that, that

00:43:06.030 --> 00:43:10.405
have, the say Asian companies have said
come to the United States.

00:43:10.405 --> 00:43:13.650
Cuz one of the reasons they did that was
they were taking all the

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.100
wealth back home, so there's a lot

00:43:15.100 --> 00:43:16.600
of encouragement for them to operate here
too.

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:20.660
But I love that Ford is in this place.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:23.520
I think that what we've really done is
accelerated Henry's

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:27.230
original vision, actually accelerated by
operating everywhere around the world.

00:43:27.230 --> 00:43:29.240
It's so neat to be part of the fabric,
plus

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:31.590
you know all the customers, you know
everything that's going on.

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:33.710
And you're, you're not only providing
great cars

00:43:33.710 --> 00:43:36.220
and trucks but also great jobs and
careers.

00:43:36.220 --> 00:43:40.190
So, I, I think the big companies that,
that have the

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:43.480
scale, and that's part of competing, are
gonna operate like global companies.

00:43:47.970 --> 00:43:49.332
&gt;&gt; Guy with the mic.

00:43:49.332 --> 00:43:50.987
&gt;&gt; My name is Guy with the mic [SOUND.]

00:43:52.170 --> 00:43:54.240
I'm Chen, first year, Alan great to have
you here.

00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:57.208
My question is about, I'm Chinese too so
you owe me a hug.

00:43:57.208 --> 00:43:58.418
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:43:58.418 --> 00:43:59.787
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:43:59.787 --> 00:44:03.970
My question is actually about jobs.

00:44:03.970 --> 00:44:08.131
So things I wanted to point out, you
caught 40% of the, the workforce at Ford.

00:44:08.131 --> 00:44:09.918
And, and you know, I think it's an

00:44:09.918 --> 00:44:14.210
understatement, given the overcapacity and
other industry in general.

00:44:14.210 --> 00:44:16.929
But you know, I think so much about the
debate about economic

00:44:16.929 --> 00:44:20.051
growth right now is about bringing jobs
back, are those jobs coming back?

00:44:20.051 --> 00:44:22.740
And do you see the auto industry grow as
bringing the jobs back?

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:26.590
&gt;&gt; Absolutely and we just announced, well
can I come back up just a

00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:29.700
little bit to your, to your start of your
question, because it's really important?

00:44:30.900 --> 00:44:34.980
This is about business and, this is about
profitability growing.

00:44:34.980 --> 00:44:38.330
And you've got to do whatever it takes,
because if, just like the situation we

00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:40.800
described, you couldn't go very many years

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:43.210
losing $17 billion, and then you're gone,
right?

00:44:43.210 --> 00:44:47.030
So you have to size yourself to the real
demand no matter what.

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:49.610
Remember in the automobile industry,
because all their costs

00:44:49.610 --> 00:44:52.539
they thought were fixed, then they'd keep
their production up...

00:44:52.539 --> 00:44:55.564
Over deliver all these vehicles, just to
get the last incremental dollar,

00:44:55.564 --> 00:44:58.469
cuz if, couldn't, didn't think they could
do anything about their cost.

00:44:58.469 --> 00:45:00.912
So what we did is we went to the to the
unions

00:45:00.912 --> 00:45:04.237
and we talked about a future where we
could actually make cars in

00:45:04.237 --> 00:45:07.687
the United States, but we had to have our
cost structure right and

00:45:07.687 --> 00:45:11.690
we had to size ourselves in the near term,
to the lower demand.

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:14.770
Well, we agreed, because you, we came
together around this vision

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:18.230
of profit growing and actually hiring,
hiring and providing great jobs.

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:20.320
But you gotta take the tough action to get
yourself

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:23.200
started so you can keep investing during
the toughest of times.

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:24.840
So and it's hard.

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:27.130
I mean it's, you don't, you don't any way
want to go through that.

00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:30.360
That's why, this economic development
worldwide is so important.

00:45:30.360 --> 00:45:34.370
Now having said that we sized ourself to
the real demand.

00:45:34.370 --> 00:45:36.240
We took out all the extra production both,

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:39.260
as somebody mentioned earlier also with
our distribution channel.

00:45:39.260 --> 00:45:41.430
And with Ford and with our suppliers.

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:44.240
And so now we're operating profitably at
the lower demand.

00:45:44.240 --> 00:45:46.880
And, and we invest it for these new
products.

00:45:46.880 --> 00:45:48.470
So now, we just announced over the next

00:45:48.470 --> 00:45:51.880
two years, we're gonna hire 7,000, new
employees.

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:55.420
And just give you one example, we're
making the new Explorer in Chicago.

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:58.250
That's the one that's 30% more efficient
than our last one.

00:45:58.250 --> 00:46:03.800
And we'll be exporting that vehicle to 94
countries around the world.

00:46:03.800 --> 00:46:06.790
And in that plant alone we'll be adding
1,200 jobs and

00:46:06.790 --> 00:46:11.920
then another 600 jobs with 100 suppliers
throughout the United States.

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:14.640
And all of our suppliers now are all
global.

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:18.530
Cuz, which, when you operate this way you
use your scale globally but

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:22.250
then you operate locally so then you get
the best of both both worlds.

00:46:22.250 --> 00:46:26.560
So, and our plan is to fundamentally now,
to grow the business

00:46:26.560 --> 00:46:30.450
year after year forever cuz that's, that's
the purpose of a business.

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:33.017
So, we're really excited about how
everybody

00:46:33.017 --> 00:46:35.080
pulled together to get to this place.

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:38.460
And now it's really exciting now to grow
and provide all these great opportunities.

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:40.420
In Asia Pacific, we're grow, we're one

00:46:40.420 --> 00:46:42.320
of the fastest growing companies in Asia
Pacific.

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:44.200
Find tremendous opportunities.

00:46:44.200 --> 00:46:47.510
Great market in Europe, a little bit
slower growth, a little bit more mature.

00:46:47.510 --> 00:46:49.620
Fabulous growth in Brazil, Russia.

00:46:49.620 --> 00:46:52.110
And especially in India and China.

00:46:54.190 --> 00:46:57.480
Okay, maybe one last question because I
hear that

00:46:57.480 --> 00:47:01.780
when it gets close, you get up and leave.

00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:01.950
&gt;&gt; Here.

00:47:01.950 --> 00:47:02.610
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:07.720
&gt;&gt; My name is Pragosh, I was at the
consumer electronic show at Las Vegas.

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:08.590
&gt;&gt; Were you really?

00:47:08.590 --> 00:47:11.750
And, I got to check out your, your Ford
Sync.

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:12.570
&gt;&gt; What'd you think?

00:47:12.570 --> 00:47:14.970
&gt;&gt; And, I love the voice activated
experience, but I

00:47:14.970 --> 00:47:17.370
was wondering, are you guys thinking about
a app store?

00:47:17.370 --> 00:47:20.630
Much like how Apple came out with theirs,
for their hardware.

00:47:20.630 --> 00:47:20.850
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:23.700
&gt;&gt; Are you coming out with an app store
for your hardware?

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:29.150
&gt;&gt; We're, we're not, right now, but you
saw the apps that we were managing now.

00:47:29.150 --> 00:47:31.740
And, we also have all the app producers
that

00:47:31.740 --> 00:47:33.970
are working on what's makes sense for a
car.

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:37.460
The real thing we decided was, was to not
get in that as a business

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:39.550
like Apple did but, but to focus

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:41.890
on the automobile and the safe driving
experience.

00:47:41.890 --> 00:47:44.780
So we're not making any money on all those
apps ourselves.

00:47:44.780 --> 00:47:46.020
We could have taken a, a piece of each

00:47:46.020 --> 00:47:48.350
of those like other business models that
you know of.

00:47:48.350 --> 00:47:50.440
Because what we, what we want to do is a
ha,

00:47:50.440 --> 00:47:53.130
was to make sure that everybody when they
bought a Ford.

00:47:53.130 --> 00:47:58.740
That they could seamlessly operate and
yet, be connected to a digital world.

00:47:58.740 --> 00:48:00.650
So we're managing that interface that you
saw with

00:48:00.650 --> 00:48:03.380
sync and My Ford and that's been our, our
focus.

00:48:03.380 --> 00:48:05.630
Now a lot of people have wanted this, you
know

00:48:05.630 --> 00:48:08.840
when Pandora comes in or any, you know,
other applications.

00:48:08.840 --> 00:48:10.520
They would like, a lot would like us to do

00:48:10.520 --> 00:48:13.070
a business with them but right now we're
focused on providing

00:48:13.070 --> 00:48:16.260
a better driving experience and a safer
experience than having

00:48:16.260 --> 00:48:19.130
all of us have access to the internet for
right now.

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:20.810
Yes.

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:23.870
&gt;&gt; Thanks again for joining us and I

00:48:23.870 --> 00:48:27.690
appreciate getting to ask the last [COUGH]
question.

00:48:27.690 --> 00:48:31.450
My name is Caroline Mellon, I'm a first
year here and I had the honor

00:48:31.450 --> 00:48:33.580
while I was at Deutsche Bank, before

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:36.290
Stanford of working with the Ford Credit
team.

00:48:36.290 --> 00:48:37.890
Oh, very good.

00:48:37.890 --> 00:48:43.490
I wanted to know what you thought their
role was in the

00:48:43.490 --> 00:48:47.220
turnaround of Ford in 2009 and their
active, kind of intensive balance

00:48:47.220 --> 00:48:49.330
sheet management, and then what your

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:51.330
objectives are for balance sheet
management

00:48:51.330 --> 00:48:56.910
as your positioning for growth, but also
preparing for another potential crisis.

00:48:56.910 --> 00:48:57.730
&gt;&gt; Fantastic.

00:48:57.730 --> 00:49:00.658
It sounded like you had a great experience
at the bank, too.

00:49:00.658 --> 00:49:01.187
[LAUGH]

00:49:01.187 --> 00:49:05.410
&gt;&gt; They're, they're, they're one of our
main bankers, too.

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:06.890
And they're fantastic.

00:49:06.890 --> 00:49:12.320
What what she is referring to is what we
call Ford Motor Credit Corporation.

00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:14.865
And it's a Captive Finance Company that we
have.

00:49:14.865 --> 00:49:17.112
Meaning that we own it.

00:49:17.112 --> 00:49:20.470
And it's cost the Capital goes with the
parent with Ford.

00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:22.980
As opposed to, you know, independent bank.

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:26.280
This has been really important for Ford,
because as you go through these

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:33.798
cycles, let's see, your banker friends
kind of come and go a little bit.

00:49:33.798 --> 00:49:36.810
and, I mean, cuz they're managing their
life too.

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:39.680
And, but, but the reason we have a, a Ford
credit

00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:42.850
corporation is that during the worst of
times, the best of times,

00:49:42.850 --> 00:49:45.720
then we can continue to help with the
financing for all of

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:48.790
our Ford store owners with all their,
their inventory, all their vehicles.

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:50.440
We also have one-stop shopping.

00:49:50.440 --> 00:49:52.250
So when you walk into a Ford store, you
can

00:49:52.250 --> 00:49:55.280
get your financing for the vehicle, in
addition to the vehicle.

00:49:55.280 --> 00:50:01.060
And remember when when we went through
this crisis GM, and they're trying to work

00:50:01.060 --> 00:50:04.160
their cash so carefully they actually sold
GMAC,

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.000
which they, which was their captive
finance company.

00:50:07.000 --> 00:50:08.800
So we really believe we've, we've done the
right thing.

00:50:08.800 --> 00:50:10.310
It really helped us get through this.

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:14.560
Right now we have scaled back Ford Credit

00:50:14.560 --> 00:50:16.450
with the divestor of all these other
brands.

00:50:16.450 --> 00:50:19.290
But now they're really right sized and
they're just

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:22.148
an absolute key part of our business going
forward.

00:50:22.148 --> 00:50:24.810
And I'm just so pleased that we keep that

00:50:24.810 --> 00:50:27.080
as a priority to keep that captive finance
company.

00:50:27.080 --> 00:50:29.940
And now on our balance sheet, now it just
gets better and better.

00:50:29.940 --> 00:50:31.370
You saw last year.

00:50:31.370 --> 00:50:35.960
That, you know, when you borrow $23.5
million, you need to pay it back, and

00:50:37.840 --> 00:50:44.234
it's a wonderful thing to be paying back
our banks, and our goal of

00:50:44.234 --> 00:50:49.730
course is to move back to investment grade
because our cost of capital goes down.

00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:53.500
That makes that allows us to invest more
in the products but also

00:50:53.500 --> 00:50:57.320
allows for credit to be even more
competitive on helping you with loans.

00:50:57.320 --> 00:51:04.060
So last year we paid $14.5 billion back on
our loans, which is fantastic and,

00:51:04.060 --> 00:51:08.460
we'll probably, keep some debt cuz, you
know, it's good to leverage, a little bit.

00:51:08.460 --> 00:51:09.970
But we're clearly moving back to
investment

00:51:09.970 --> 00:51:12.460
rate, a lot faster than what everybody
thought.

00:51:12.460 --> 00:51:14.050
And most important thing is you're growing

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:17.170
the business profitably, generating free
cash flow.

00:51:17.170 --> 00:51:18.570
And it gives you great options not only on

00:51:18.570 --> 00:51:20.300
improving the balance sheet, but also on
the products.

00:51:21.920 --> 00:51:22.720
okay.

00:51:22.720 --> 00:51:24.950
I know you're gonna get up and leave.

00:51:24.950 --> 00:51:28.030
And, and I would take it personally if I
wasn't done, so I'm gonna be done.

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:31.990
I, was honored, to get a request to come
here.

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:35.740
I, I grew up in Kansas, I grew up in
Seattle, Lebowing.

00:51:35.740 --> 00:51:39.190
We've had tremendous programs with
Stanford over the years.

00:51:39.190 --> 00:51:40.770
It's a fabulous school, as you know.

00:51:40.770 --> 00:51:43.660
You're getting, one of the finest
educations, and

00:51:43.660 --> 00:51:45.610
you're also getting one of the best global
educations.

00:51:45.610 --> 00:51:48.550
And it's so important, for where the
world's going and

00:51:48.550 --> 00:51:53.270
so, thank you for the invitation and,
congratulations, to you.

00:51:53.270 --> 00:51:54.990
I look forward to working with you.

00:51:54.990 --> 00:51:59.038
I would love you to consider maybe Ford,
as you're considering going forward.

00:51:59.038 --> 00:52:03.391
And also,

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:09.160
maybe you should drive a Ford, and feel
the difference.

00:52:11.210 --> 00:52:11.765
Thanks a lot.

